    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Osteosarcoma is an aggressive primary tumor of the bone, often presenting itself in childhood and adolescence. Treatments in the last 30 years have not improved considerably due to the genetic complexity and genomic instability of osteosarcoma. Therefore, it is necessary to consider epigenetic modifications that may contribute to osteosarcomagenesis. One set of epigenetic modifications are regulated by Ten-eleven translocation (TET) family enzymes, which oxidize 5-methylcytosines (5mC) to 5-hydroxymethylcytosines (5hmC). Current models propose that 5hmc is an intermediate base in the DNA demethylation process that operates during important reprogramming phases of mammalian development. We show that loss of TET affects osteoblast maturation by depleting 5hmc levels in the genome and can prime the epigenetic landscape of osteoblasts towards oncogenesis. Consequently, increasing TET activity in osteosarcoma patient-derived xenografts by adding its co-factor, ascorbic acid (AsA) rescues this decrease in 5hmc and abrogates cell proliferation. By investigating levels of global gene expression before and after AsA treatment, gains in 5hmc were observed in specific subsets of genes associated with apoptosis and cell cycle dynamics, which seem to mitigate the cancerous phenotype. Such results contribute to a better understanding of epigenetic regulation of osteosarcoma, and furthermore implicate AsA as a potential epigenetic therapy for osteosarcoma.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
